WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.640
SOC 2 reports are organized around Trust Services Criteria.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.660
The Security category is commonly included because nearly every SOC 2 report needs to

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.280
address protection against unauthorized

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:16.380
access, unauthorized disclosure, and damage to systems that support the service.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.920
Other categories may be included depending on the service and the commitments made to

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.100
customers.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.080
Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, and Privacy are not automatic.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.940
They should be included when they match the service, contractual promises, risk profile,

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.000
and customer expectations.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.380
Scope is where SOC 2 becomes concrete.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.840
The scope defines which service or system is included, which environments are relevant,

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.780
which people and teams operate the controls, which processes support the service,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.720
and which controls map to the selected criteria.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.980
A cloud platform, a customer-facing SaaS application, an internal support process,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.600
and a data processing workflow can all

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.740
have different scope boundaries.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.640
Good scope connects the service being sold to the promises made to customers.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.780
If customers rely on uptime commitments, availability may matter.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.480
If the service stores confidential customer information, confidentiality may matter.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.020
If the service processes transactions where completeness and accuracy are central,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.080
processing integrity may matter.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.380
If the service collects personal information under privacy commitments,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.040
privacy may matter.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.260
The scope should explain these relationships without pretending the report covers more

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.600
than it does.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.900
Weak scope creates confusion.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.880
If the report includes a control but not the system customers care about,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.580
the report may not answer the real risk question.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.200
If the report describes a service broadly but evidence covers only a small environment,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.220
the team may struggle during audit and customer review.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.040
If commitments are vague,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.940
teams may collect evidence that does not connect to the criteria.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:58.120
Scope is not just paperwork; it drives evidence, ownership, and report usefulness.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:02.340
Technical and operational teams should understand scope because their work may become

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.360
evidence.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.820
Access to production, change management for customer-facing systems,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.880
incident response for the service, vendor dependencies, backup processes, monitoring,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.220
and onboarding or offboarding may all be in scope.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.680
Teams need to know which systems and processes matter so they can operate controls

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:22.940
consistently.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.640
The practical lesson is to define scope early and keep it aligned with reality.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:33.060
Document the service, boundaries, dependencies, selected Trust Services Categories,

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.760
customer commitments, and control owners.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.420
When the scope is clear,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:42.760
Type I readiness and Type II evidence collection become much easier to manage.
